@h1 CLI reference h1@

The @code wit code@ binary is both a tool and the model host implementation.
Every embedding question — how to wire a data loader, how output paths are
chosen — is answered concretely by the CLI's source. This page is the command
reference and the @code wit.sources.json code@ schema.

@h2 Commands h2@

@ul
@li @code wit parse <file> code@ — print the AST as JSON. li@
@li @code wit check <file> code@ — parse, resolve, and expand; report errors only
(non-zero exit on failure). li@
@li @@@code wit fmt <file> [-w|--write] code@@@ — structural re-indent via
@code format() code@. li@
@li @code wit build <file> [options] code@ — compile to HTML, Markdown, or PDF
(see below). li@
@li @code wit tour <file> code@ — a narrated walkthrough of the document. li@
@li @code wit --version code@ / @code wit --help code@. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) 💡 @code wit --version code@ prints @code 0.2.0 code@, the
@code VERSION code@ constant in @code bin.ts code@, matching the published
packages. aside@

@h2 build h2@

@@@pre
wit build <file>
  [-o out.html | out.md | out.pdf]
  [--format html | md | pdf]
  [--raw | --fragment]
  [--sources wit.sources.json --allow-exec]
  [--env .env]
pre@@@

The output format is inferred from the @code -o code@ extension
(@code .html code@ or @code .htm code@ → HTML, @code .md code@ or
@code .markdown code@ → Markdown, @code .pdf code@ → PDF) or set with
@code --format code@. With no @code -o code@, the default is a styled HTML
@strong document strong@ on stdout.

@ul
@li @code --fragment code@ — emit the bare @code <article> code@ instead of a
full page. li@
@li @code --raw code@ — use @code rawThemeCss code@ (the mechanical reset) instead
of the house style. li@
@li @strong PDF strong@ — renders document-mode HTML, then paginates with a
discovered headless Chrome. @code WIT_CHROME code@ overrides the binary;
@code --no-pdf-header-footer code@ drops the running header and footer. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) ⚠️ The CLI defaults to document mode; the
@a |href /docs/build/rendering/| library a@ defaults to @code fragment code@. The
two surfaces disagree on purpose — a CLI user wants a file, a library caller
usually wants a fragment to inject. aside@

@h2 The external-data host h2@

The CLI is one implementation of the @@@code @load code@@@ seam. @code env code@
is built in — it returns the process environment (plus an optional
@code --env code@ dotenv file) as a record. Every other alias comes from
@code wit.sources.json code@:

@@@pre
{
  "sources": {
    "downloads": {
      "run": ["node", "scripts/downloads.js"],
      "format": "json",
      "timeoutMs": 30000
    }
  }
}
pre@@@

@code format code@ is one of @code json code@, @code csv code@, @code tsv code@,
@code lines code@, @code text code@, @code svg code@, or @code html code@ — seven
output formats, plus the built-in @code env code@.

@h3 The security boundary h3@

Running an aliased program requires @code --allow-exec code@. A document can name
only an @strong alias, strong@ never a command — the command lives in
@code wit.sources.json code@, which the document author does not control. That
separation is the whole security model of the data host.

@h3 How captures reach the program h3@

Two ways, both at once: @@@code {{name}} code@@@ tokens are substituted into the
@code run code@ argv, and the full capture object is written to the program's
stdin as JSON. CSV and TSV get a minimal header-keyed parse — @strong no quoted
fields. strong@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Expecting @@@code @load <alias> code@@@ to run a program without
@code --allow-exec code@ — only @code env code@ works unguarded. li@
@li Expecting quoted CSV fields to parse — the parser is minimal. li@
ul@

